WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.294 --> 00:00:02.126
The Blue Death In late September of 1918, near the end of World War I, a

2
00:00:02.127 --> 00:00:12.889
soldier was brought to the hospital at Camp Devens outside Boston, Massachusetts. He was feverish and

3
00:00:12.890 --> 00:00:19.048
aching so badly that he screamed whenever he was touched. In the days that followed, hundreds

4
00:00:19.049 --> 00:00:25.203
of more men poured into the hospital with similar symptoms. The highly contagious illness had

5
00:00:25.204 --> 00:00:33.451
swept through the large camp, almost a city itself with over fifty thousand men. Soon the

6
00:00:33.452 --> 00:00:37.689
hospital didn’t have enough beds for the soldiers. Many stood outside in the chilly air, often

7
00:00:37.690 --> 00:00:45.932
in the rain, waiting for treatment as they clutched blankets and grew weaker. In the month

8
00:00:45.933 --> 00:00:53.186
of September alone, more than twelve thousand soldiers were reported as having the disease. Most of

9
00:00:53.187 --> 00:01:03.759
the sick soldiers at Camp Devens recovered. But according to one of the doctors working there,

10
00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:11.149
approximately a hundred men a day came down with pneumonia. Their lungs filled with blood, which

11
00:01:11.150 --> 00:01:20.841
poured from their noses and mouths, and they literally drowned to death. People soon began calling

12
00:01:20.842 --> 00:01:31.393
the disease the Blue Death because victims' skin often turned blue, an indication that death

13
00:01:31.394 --> 00:01:40.720
was near. Meanwhile these soldiers at Camp Devens—some in the early stages of the illness, before

14
00:01:40.721 --> 00:01:46.625
it could be detected—were being shipped overseas to fight. On the battlefields of Europe and Asia,

15
00:01:46.626 --> 00:01:49.466
the Blue Death spread like wildfire, soon infecting the civilian population as well. A pandemic, or

16
00:01:49.467 --> 00:02:04.610
worldwide epidemic, had begun. "In Flew Enza" Most influenza pandemics start in Asia. But the 1918

17
00:02:04.611 --> 00:02:09.773
pandemic originated in three countries—the United States, France, and southern China—and spread in waves from each

18
00:02:09.774 --> 00:02:15.193
of these places. Some experts think that half of the world population was eventually infected. In

19
00:02:15.194 --> 00:02:21.867
the United States about 600,000 people lost their lives. India was hit particularly hard, with

20
00:02:21.868 --> 00:02:29.516
some estimates of nearly twenty million deaths. Even remote areas were not immune; villages in the

21
00:02:29.517 --> 00:02:35.349
far reaches of Alaska were completely wiped out. The 1918 flu epidemic is also known as

22
00:02:35.350 --> 00:02:39.853
the Spanish influenza. Spain was a politically neutral country during World War I. This meant that

23
00:02:39.854 --> 00:02:45.590
Spanish newspapers were not censored, as they were in other countries. So Spain was the first

24
00:02:45.591 --> 00:02:52.749
nation to report on the dangerous new flu attacking its population. The Spanish influenza epidemic was

25
00:02:52.750 --> 00:03:05.238
unusual in one key way: most of the infected people were between twenty and forty years

26
00:03:05.239 --> 00:03:10.235
of age. Children were frequent victims too. Many kids got the disease themselves, or lost

27
00:03:10.236 --> 00:03:18.728
their parents from it. The illness struck so many households in the United States, a popular

28
00:03:18.729 --> 00:03:22.971
jump-rope rhyme soon acquired chilling new words: I had a little bird And its name was

29
00:03:22.972 --> 00:03:27.475
Enza. I opened the window And in-flew-Enza. Dangerous Misconceptions One reason the epidemic caused so much

30
00:03:27.476 --> 00:03:30.039
illness and death was that people did not understand how contagious it was. It was wartime

31
00:03:30.040 --> 00:03:37.713
so people were traveling to different countries and bringing the illness with them. And people with

32
00:03:37.714 --> 00:03:44.030
mild cases of the flu thought they just had colds so they went to work or

33
00:03:44.031 --> 00:03:53.439
school, which helped the virus spread further. As more people grew seriously ill, public health departments

34
00:03:53.440 --> 00:04:02.274
reacted strongly, and fear turned into panic. Schools and workplaces were closed, and public gatherings

35
00:04:02.275 --> 00:04:10.006
such as church services and parades were banned. Throughout the world people wore gauze masks to

36
00:04:10.007 --> 00:04:16.855
try to protect themselves from infection. But the masks didn't do much to stop the disease

37
00:04:16.856 --> 00:04:20.918
from spreading. People wearing them could still breathe in small particles of the influenza virus. Many

38
00:04:20.919 --> 00:04:26.419
people also relied on folk remedies to keep infection away. They hung garlic from their necks,

39
00:04:26.420 --> 00:04:31.329
left windows wide open in winter, and even stuffed salt up their noses! Some Americans used

40
00:04:31.330 --> 00:04:39.489
laxatives as a treatment for the flu, while others turned to addictive substances such as alcohol.

41
00:04:39.490 --> 00:04:47.736
Public health officials did their best to try to manage the crisis. But with neither

42
00:04:47.737 --> 00:04:52.374
a vaccine nor any reliable treatments, all they could do was focus on educating people about

43
00:04:52.375 --> 00:05:01.297
prevention. They printed posters, cartoons, and pamphlets that offered advice on how to stay healthy. But

44
00:05:01.298 --> 00:05:07.564
even these government documents contained misinformation. According to one pamphlet, people could keep the flu away

45
00:05:07.565 --> 00:05:11.705
by chewing food carefully and making sure their shoes weren't too tight! What is Influenza? Influenza

46
00:05:11.706 --> 00:05:20.706
is a highly contagious disease caused by the influenza virus. The symptoms of the flu—fever, body

47
00:05:20.707 --> 00:05:29.287
aches, coughing—pass within two weeks, but if the virus infects the lungs, pneumonia can develop. While

48
00:05:29.288 --> 00:05:40.019
pneumonia is usually treatable, it can lead to death. Seasonal flu viruses occur each year, usually

49
00:05:40.020 --> 00:05:44.932
during the winter. Influenza pandemics, however, occur only a few times each century, and they

50
00:05:44.933 --> 00:05:50.023
behave differently than outbreaks of seasonal flu. Influenza pandemics involve a new strain of the flu

51
00:05:50.024 --> 00:05:59.088
virus, one to which people haven't built up any immunity. The new virus tends to strike

52
00:05:59.089 --> 00:06:06.668
harder, and with worse symptoms. Vaccines are developed each year to treat seasonal flu, but in

53
00:06:06.669 --> 00:06:11.577
the case of a pandemic, it can take longer to develop a vaccine. In the meantime

54
00:06:11.578 --> 00:06:23.735
many people can die. Two other pandemics, the Russian flu of 1889 and the Asian flu

55
00:06:23.736 --> 00:06:32.991
of 1957, together killed millions. The 1918 Spanish influenza was by far the worst in recent

56
00:06:32.992 --> 00:06:42.986
history, killing from twenty to fifty million people worldwide. More people died from the Spanish

57
00:06:42.987 --> 00:06:48.556
flu than from fighting in World War I. Flu Prevention In the years since 1918, great

58
00:06:48.557 --> 00:06:50.452
strides have been made in understanding influenza. Scientists have developed vaccines for the seasonal flu, and

59
00:06:50.453 --> 00:06:56.801
the public is better informed about how to cope with it. In 2009 a new pandemic—caused

60
00:06:56.802 --> 00:07:05.303
by the H1N1, or "swine flu," virus—made its appearance, and researchers immediately rushed to educate people

61
00:07:05.304 --> 00:07:19.706
and develop vaccines. Many people, especially children and young adults, were immunized by local health agencies.

62
00:07:19.707 --> 00:07:26.443
Despite predictions of a massive spread of H1N1, experts announced in December 2009 that the pandemic

63
00:07:26.444 --> 00:07:36.604
had been milder than expected. There are some simple things each person can do to

64
00:07:36.605 --> 00:07:44.351
prevent the flu: to begin with, get the seasonal flu vaccine. If you are in a

65
00:07:44.352 --> 00:07:50.016
risk group for the H1N1 virus, get that vaccine too. Wash your hands frequently with soap

66
00:07:50.017 --> 00:07:55.751
and water, or an alcohol-based hand rub. Cover your nose and mouth with a tissue when

67
00:07:55.752 --> 00:08:02.144
you sneeze—and then discard the tissue! Avoid touching your eyes, nose, and mouth, as this can

68
00:08:02.145 --> 00:08:08.223
spread germs. After the Crisis Eventually life at Camp Devens in the United States returned to

69
00:08:08.224 --> 00:08:10.212
normal. In January of 1919, some soldiers even entertained others at the camp by putting on

70
00:08:10.213 --> 00:08:15.220
a musical play, C’est la guerre (That’s War), about life in the military. By then the

71
00:08:15.221 --> 00:08:27.630
worst of the crisis had passed at the camp, and the soldiers were trying to

72
00:08:27.631 --> 00:08:35.376
put the grim time behind them. There is no guarantee that a pandemic as devastating as

73
00:08:35.377 --> 00:08:42.711
the 1918 Spanish flu won’t occur again. But with our advanced understanding of the influenza virus,

74
00:08:42.712 --> 00:08:55.856
and our ability to quickly develop vaccines, we now hold many more tools for preventing it.

